  "prediction": "**CBS News Overhauls \"The Early Show\" with New Team**\n\nIn a bid to boost ratings, CBS News has given its morning show \"The Early Show\" a complete makeover, replacing its co-hosts and weather forecaster with a new team. The changes, which take effect on January 3, aim to revitalize the show, which has consistently ranked third in the network morning news ratings behind NBC's \"Today\" show and ABC's \"Good Morning America.\"\n\n**New Team Members**\n\nThe new co-anchors of \"The Early Show\" will be Chris Wragge and Erica Hill, who have been anchoring the Saturday edition of the show since 2008. Wragge, a sports journalist, has been working at CBS since 2006, while Hill, a CNN veteran, has been filling in for Maggie Rodriguez on the show. Jeff Glor, who has been anchoring the network's Saturday newscast, will join the team as the regular news reader. Marysol Castro, a weather anchor from ABC's \"Good Morning America\" weekend edition, will serve as the new weather forecaster.\n\n**Departing Team Members**\n\nMaggie Rodriguez, Harry Smith, and Dave Price are leaving the show. Smith, who has been with the show since 2002, will become the primary substitute anchor for Katie Couric's evening newscast, \"Face the Nation,\" and \"Sunday Morning.\" Rodriguez and Price will be discussing future roles at the network.\n\n**Ratings Expectations**\n\nCBS News President Sean McManus is optimistic about the new team's potential to grow the show's ratings, but acknowledges that it may take time. The show has consistently ranked behind \"Today\" and \"Good Morning America\" in the morning news ratings.\n\n**Reaction from Critics**\n\nVeteran television critic David Bianculli believes that the sudden changes are a sign of desperation, but acknowledges that the show has not been working well in its current format. He praises the new team's chemistry and experience, but notes that it will be a challenge to break the show out of third place.\n\nOverall, the changes aim to revitalize \"The Early Show\" and make it more competitive in the morning news ratings. Only time will tell if the new team will be able to achieve this goal.",
  "answers": [
    "CBS is scrapping its Early Show team, replacing co-hosts Harry Smith and Maggie Rodriguez with the anchors of the show's Saturday edition, Chris Wragge and Erica Hill. Other more minor players also are leaving in the switch, which takes place early next year, the LA Times reports. The show has been consistently stuck in third place, well behind Today and Good Morning America. Smith, who survived the last major overhaul of the show in 2002, will stick with the network as Katie Couric's main backup on the CBS Evening News, notes AP. Click here for more."
